WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.445
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:09.390
DONALD CLARK: Myself and my two
colleagues, Dave and Manasa,

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.620
are going to talk you through
a few different aspects

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.990
of Google's network and
why we think it makes

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:15.820
for a high performance network.

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:20.780
My section is going
to be taking you

00:00:20.780 --> 00:00:22.850
through the basics,
the underlying

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:26.180
physical and operating aspects
of Google, Google's network

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:28.375
that powers Google Cloud.

00:00:28.375 --> 00:00:29.750
Going to talk a
little bit how we

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:34.300
plan to build our network for
resilience, how we operate

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:37.700
in a resilient fashion, and
also how we extend this thinking

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:39.620
beyond the boundaries
of our own network

00:00:39.620 --> 00:00:42.740
to the internet in general.

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:44.570
So let's start off.

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:48.550
It's possibly no surprise that
Google owns, and operates,

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:52.270
and has built probably one
of the largest networks

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.310
in the world.

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.920
This network is
primarily based on fiber

00:00:56.920 --> 00:00:59.590
that we've let
ourselves or we've even

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:03.150
built ourselves or in
conjunction with partners.

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:05.170
Now, what this
means is that we can

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:08.740
offer a predictable and
resilient performance

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:11.870
level all across the world.

00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:14.140
So, if we look at the map,
you can see here these

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.598
are all the network
past that we've

00:01:15.598 --> 00:01:19.960
built over the 20 or so years
that Google has been running.

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.080
Highlighted as well here we
have some of our major submarine

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:26.257
cable investments dating
back to 2010 with Unity,

00:01:26.257 --> 00:01:28.840
all the way through to the more
recently announced cables that

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:32.420
are coming on stream
in the coming year.

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:36.910
So this is the network that
enables us to serve billions

00:01:36.910 --> 00:01:38.690
of search query answers.

00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:41.080
This is the network
that enables us

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:44.170
to serve billions of
hours of YouTube videos

00:01:44.170 --> 00:01:46.670
to users all over the world.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:49.150
And this is also the
network that Google

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:53.320
uses to replicate content and
services between our custom

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.750
built data centers
on our footprint

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:57.940
again across the world.

00:01:57.940 --> 00:01:59.950
And, critically,
it's the network

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:01.600
that powers Google Cloud.

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:08.310
Now, in recent years, what's
interesting and exciting

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.740
for us in the Cloud
team is that it's

00:02:10.740 --> 00:02:13.360
our cloud customers and
some of their demands

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:16.590
that have started to shape
the nature of our network

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:20.070
investments and network planning
as we go to new locations

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:23.180
with new mini clusters
a new cloud regions,

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:25.450
and as we have to think
careful about how we connect

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:29.500
in ways that prioritize maybe
latency over resilience.

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.090
Let me give you an
example of that.

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.490
This is last year.

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:36.700
And you'll see here
that when we built

00:02:36.700 --> 00:02:40.120
some new paths between
Singapore, our Southeast Asia

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:46.640
region, and Sydney, we almost
cut our latency in half.

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:48.220
It's actually not
possible to get

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:51.130
that much shorter latency now
between these two locations.

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:51.940
OK.

00:02:51.940 --> 00:02:54.730
And this is a great
example of where we've

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.310
said latency is important.

00:02:57.310 --> 00:02:58.580
Resilience is important.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:00.550
Let's build more
capacity that makes

00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:01.690
sense for our cloud users.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:06.430
And we're doubling
down in this region.

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:10.150
Later this year we will have
two new cable investments,

00:03:10.150 --> 00:03:12.970
the Indigo cable
and the JGA cable

00:03:12.970 --> 00:03:16.630
coming on stream to add
even more capacity and more

00:03:16.630 --> 00:03:18.870
performance.

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:20.580
What's also interesting
to think about

00:03:20.580 --> 00:03:26.440
is that Google is now at
a scale in its operations

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:30.030
that it can go about
planning and building

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.910
submarine cables by itself.

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:36.490
What this means is
that we can provide

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.350
a level of path certainty.

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:39.900
We can lay the
cable where we need

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.610
it to go to connect our
locations that makes sense

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:44.320
for Google and for cloud.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:46.640
And we can do it
an awful lot faster

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:48.640
than traditional approaches
where you're working

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:50.780
with many consortia partners.

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:53.080
So these things are
called private cables

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.955
in the industry parlance.

00:03:54.955 --> 00:03:57.580
If you want to read a little bit
more about some private cables

00:03:57.580 --> 00:04:00.700
that we've announced, encourage
you to use your favorite search

00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:04.420
engine and look up the Curie
cable which is going from Chile

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.598
up the west coast into North
America or the Dunant cable,

00:04:08.598 --> 00:04:11.140
which there is a press release
about some exciting technology

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:13.480
Dave will talk about
just last week.

00:04:13.480 --> 00:04:15.850
But the Dunant cable goes
between France and America

00:04:15.850 --> 00:04:16.399
as well.

00:04:16.399 --> 00:04:18.060
And these are
private cable systems

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:20.012
Google is building by itself.

00:04:20.012 --> 00:04:21.970
And we're able to do that
because of our scale.

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:27.730
Now the great news for all
of you here as Google Cloud

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:31.960
customers is that you're able to
connect to this network at 134

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:34.210
locations all around the world.

00:04:34.210 --> 00:04:37.630
That could be directly or
through an extensive network

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:40.430
of Cloud Interconnect partners.

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:44.140
It has never been easier to get
on board our global network.

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:49.450
Now it's great having
a huge network,

00:04:49.450 --> 00:04:52.920
but as our traffic grows, and
we build new cloud regions,

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.790
and we have to build new
paths for new capacity,

00:04:55.790 --> 00:04:58.718
it's also critical that
we maintain and improve

00:04:58.718 --> 00:05:00.010
the performance of the network.

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:05.680
Now when we're planning,
we use extensive simulation

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:08.560
whenever we introduce
or consider introducing

00:05:08.560 --> 00:05:09.640
new network capacity.

00:05:12.092 --> 00:05:13.550
We want to make
sure that it is not

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:16.400
going to impact
our SLOs negatively

00:05:16.400 --> 00:05:18.932
or it's not going to do
so in unexpected ways.

00:05:18.932 --> 00:05:20.390
And as part of
this, we've actually

00:05:20.390 --> 00:05:22.580
evolved our planning processes.

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:27.080
So we used to use trended
historical data on outages

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:29.830
and expected failure rates.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:32.080
And we'd run some Monte
Carlo type simulations

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:34.420
to actually understand what
the potential SLO would

00:05:34.420 --> 00:05:36.580
expect to see was.

00:05:36.580 --> 00:05:38.410
Now we've moved beyond
that a little bit.

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:40.690
And we're performing more
discrete event analysis

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:41.540
these days.

00:05:41.540 --> 00:05:44.170
So what we do is we
classify a whole batch

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:48.160
of different failure types
across the entire network path.

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:50.620
And we assign each of
them probability curves

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:53.440
and frequency curves of
happening, blast radius,

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:55.570
independency variables.

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:57.760
We then push these all
together and run them

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:02.470
for a fictional 10,
20, 30, 50 years

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:05.110
into the future against a whole
different range of network

00:06:05.110 --> 00:06:06.640
topologies.

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:10.750
And what this does is gives
us a reasonably high degree

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:13.180
of certainty that
the network we choose

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:15.490
and the additional capacity
locations and paths

00:06:15.490 --> 00:06:18.580
that we add into it is
going to meet, and maintain,

00:06:18.580 --> 00:06:21.550
and increase the resilience
and the predictability

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:24.180
that we care so much about.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:26.400
This sounds like
a lot of bother.

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.770
Is it really worth it?

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:30.710
The answer is
really clearly yes.

00:06:30.710 --> 00:06:31.210
OK.

00:06:31.210 --> 00:06:35.190
Here's an example
from 2018 last year.

00:06:35.190 --> 00:06:39.570
We're looking here at two data
centers in our APAC region.

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:41.160
We have a number of paths.

00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:44.520
I've obfuscated the data here
slightly for commercial reasons

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:45.830
on the cables.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:51.870
But you can see we had two
cuts, two jewel case failure

00:06:51.870 --> 00:06:56.213
scenarios where we lost
paths on two systems at once.

00:06:56.213 --> 00:06:57.630
And in fact, one
of these was what

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:00.760
we call a jewel
case worst failure

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:02.510
where we lost one of
the very large paths.

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:07.780
These are also quite
long, enduring events.

00:07:07.780 --> 00:07:10.030
And that's because in
the submarine world,

00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:11.980
the actual identification
of a failure

00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:15.160
through to the rectification
can take months.

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:16.150
You have to permit.

00:07:16.150 --> 00:07:17.710
There's weather issues.

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:21.190
The ship may not be available
to go and do the repair.

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:22.660
It may not find the cable.

00:07:22.660 --> 00:07:25.980
It may have to
remake the repair.

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:28.750
Now, fortunately, because of
all of that planning and effort

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:31.210
into building a resilient
network that I talked

00:07:31.210 --> 00:07:35.620
about earlier, no Google Cloud
customer and no Google service

00:07:35.620 --> 00:07:39.220
was negatively impacted by
these jewel case failure events.

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:40.000
OK.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:41.200
Things just went on.

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:42.980
And that's what we
try and aim for.

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:45.868
We try very much to not
leave anything to chance

00:07:45.868 --> 00:07:47.410
when we're building
out this network.

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:52.650
So now that we've built
it in a resilient manner,

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:55.850
it's critical that we operate
it resiliently as well.

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:57.225
And there's three
aspects to that

00:07:57.225 --> 00:07:59.350
that I want to share
with you today.

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:02.550
The first is SDN everywhere.

00:08:02.550 --> 00:08:05.700
It's no secret that we've
operated an SDN network

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:08.910
on our core inter-data
center campus network.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:10.380
However a couple
of years ago, we

00:08:10.380 --> 00:08:13.320
announced with Espresso
that we were pushing SDN

00:08:13.320 --> 00:08:14.610
right out to the edge.

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:18.860
And that process is effectively
finished as off now.

00:08:18.860 --> 00:08:19.740
What does this mean?

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:23.240
Well, it enables us to
maintain a level of performance

00:08:23.240 --> 00:08:26.450
and availability right out to
the edge of the network that's

00:08:26.450 --> 00:08:29.630
simply not possible using
regular routing approaches

00:08:29.630 --> 00:08:31.710
and routing protocols.

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:35.440
We can optimize our link
utilization at the same time

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:38.419
as we maintain certain business
rules, quality of service

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:40.280
aspects, and priorities.

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:42.260
It also offers a lot
faster convergence

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:45.455
than traditional protocols
like MPLS running RSVP.

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:51.240
Secondly, at the
edge of our network,

00:08:51.240 --> 00:08:55.380
we connect with nearly all
significant network operators.

00:08:55.380 --> 00:08:59.820
And this allows us to hand
off directly and reliably

00:08:59.820 --> 00:09:05.400
close to the end user
about 99% of our traffic.

00:09:05.400 --> 00:09:07.710
So together these
first two points

00:09:07.710 --> 00:09:10.230
translate to higher
availability,

00:09:10.230 --> 00:09:13.200
better performance through
Google's Cloud network

00:09:13.200 --> 00:09:18.140
than is available through
the internet at large.

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:21.160
But the icing on the cake
is always our SRE teams,

00:09:21.160 --> 00:09:23.530
so our site reliability
engineering teams.

00:09:23.530 --> 00:09:25.810
If you've not visited
the SRE lounge yet,

00:09:25.810 --> 00:09:27.400
it's across in the back corner.

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:30.310
Look for the inflatable unicorn.

00:09:30.310 --> 00:09:32.890
Google literally
wrote the book on SRE

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:36.430
and it's available in the
bookshop in the Swank corner.

00:09:36.430 --> 00:09:38.380
We have a number of
network SRE teams

00:09:38.380 --> 00:09:40.580
stationed throughout the world.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:44.290
And it's their role to
continually respond and adapt

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:48.610
to network events to improve
systems and processes so

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:50.770
that the reliability and
performance of the network

00:09:50.770 --> 00:09:53.122
continues to improve over time.

00:09:53.122 --> 00:09:54.580
It's a classic
post-mortem culture.

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:57.340
If something goes wrong,
we don't blame people.

00:09:57.340 --> 00:09:59.020
We identify root cause.

00:09:59.020 --> 00:10:01.780
We learn and we implement
approaches and workarounds

00:10:01.780 --> 00:10:04.390
to build a resilience
and a performance that

00:10:04.390 --> 00:10:07.630
only goes one way, up.

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:09.880
Our SRE teams are
also on hand to give

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:13.570
a second level of support
to our customer facing staff

00:10:13.570 --> 00:10:16.480
and help on some deep
cloud issues that

00:10:16.480 --> 00:10:18.460
may require a little
bit more involvement

00:10:18.460 --> 00:10:20.728
on the actual network layer.

00:10:20.728 --> 00:10:22.270
So together we
operate for resilience

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:23.440
across these three levels.

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:30.790
But our network, your
traffic, our traffic,

00:10:30.790 --> 00:10:34.276
doesn't stop at the
edge of our network.

00:10:34.276 --> 00:10:35.500
OK.

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:38.770
The internet is a critical
part of how all of us

00:10:38.770 --> 00:10:42.210
deliver traffic to end
users to our customers.

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:44.200
And it will continue to be so.

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:47.020
So our commitment to
performance and to reliability

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:49.540
and trustworthiness goes
beyond the edge of our network

00:10:49.540 --> 00:10:52.210
to the internet at large.

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:54.010
Now as many of you
know, the reality

00:10:54.010 --> 00:10:55.900
is the internet is not
a controlled space.

00:10:55.900 --> 00:10:58.360
Bad stuff happens.

00:10:58.360 --> 00:11:01.140
Issues like right hijacks
are a fact of life.

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:02.410
OK.

00:11:02.410 --> 00:11:07.240
Unfortunately, a keyboard
error by a small ISP in Africa

00:11:07.240 --> 00:11:09.870
can really cause
global problems.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:13.340
As maybe some of you remember
from news articles last year,

00:11:13.340 --> 00:11:15.470
they hit Google.

00:11:15.470 --> 00:11:16.530
So that's not great.

00:11:16.530 --> 00:11:18.630
So here's an example.

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:21.907
We've got a bad actor.

00:11:21.907 --> 00:11:23.990
They're doing a couple of
things that's not ideal.

00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:25.350
Let's assume it's not malicious.

00:11:25.350 --> 00:11:26.960
It's just a keyboard mistake.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:28.813
Anyone could make it.

00:11:28.813 --> 00:11:30.980
They're going to advertise
some of Google's IP space

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:32.730
towards their peers.

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:34.700
They're also advertising
some IP space

00:11:34.700 --> 00:11:37.760
from another network out
towards the internet.

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:43.030
Well, today these routes are
going to start to propagate

00:11:43.030 --> 00:11:45.020
and traffic could
go the wrong way.

00:11:45.020 --> 00:11:45.520
OK.

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:48.250
User traffic could end
up in black holes, being

00:11:48.250 --> 00:11:51.700
isolated, just bad things.

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:52.660
So this is not great.

00:11:52.660 --> 00:11:55.390
And we've seen this
happen too many times.

00:11:55.390 --> 00:11:57.150
What's Google trying
to do about this?

00:11:57.150 --> 00:11:57.650
OK.

00:11:57.650 --> 00:12:02.320
Well, firstly, we're continually
enhancing our own monitoring

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:04.000
and filtering capabilities.

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:07.030
We want to make sure we
can get quicker and better

00:12:07.030 --> 00:12:11.820
at identifying these sorts of
issues and responding to them.

00:12:11.820 --> 00:12:15.360
However, this is a
community wide issue.

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:19.580
And so we are working with other
organizations in partnership.

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:20.623
We've joined MANRS.

00:12:20.623 --> 00:12:22.290
For those of you
who've not heard of it,

00:12:22.290 --> 00:12:23.957
is the best acronym
in the world, MANRS,

00:12:23.957 --> 00:12:27.260
the Mutually Agreed Norms
for Routing Security.

00:12:27.260 --> 00:12:29.880
It is an ISOC sponsored group.

00:12:29.880 --> 00:12:32.450
And it's really working
to try and enhance

00:12:32.450 --> 00:12:34.580
the security of routing
on the internet.

00:12:34.580 --> 00:12:36.440
So Google has recently joined.

00:12:36.440 --> 00:12:38.090
And I can announce
that shortly we

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:41.360
will start applying
routing filters

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:44.270
based on the routing
registry IRR to all

00:12:44.270 --> 00:12:46.460
of our public peering sessions.

00:12:46.460 --> 00:12:49.640
So we want to slowly encourage
other network operators

00:12:49.640 --> 00:12:52.940
to up their game in maintaining
the accuracy and veracity

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:55.607
of those IRR records.

00:12:55.607 --> 00:12:58.190
Now that's great, but it's going
to take a little bit of time.

00:12:58.190 --> 00:12:59.810
Anything on the
internet takes time.

00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:02.880
Hopefully, this will be
faster than IPv6 everywhere.

00:13:02.880 --> 00:13:05.780
So the other thing we're doing
is something called peer lock.

00:13:05.780 --> 00:13:08.210
And so peer lock we think
will give us some short term

00:13:08.210 --> 00:13:09.560
immediate gain.

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:11.720
And we're going to work
with about 15 or 20

00:13:11.720 --> 00:13:15.350
of our largest peering
partners to effectively bring

00:13:15.350 --> 00:13:18.920
an additional level of filtering
safety, which will validate

00:13:18.920 --> 00:13:21.230
not just the addresses
but also the paths that we

00:13:21.230 --> 00:13:24.420
wish to receive
those addresses from.

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:26.790
So together with
these two aspects,

00:13:26.790 --> 00:13:29.280
we believe that a lot of
these negative situations

00:13:29.280 --> 00:13:30.630
will be blocked.

00:13:30.630 --> 00:13:32.820
It's going to bring
additional valuable protection

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:35.140
and stability, not
just to Google traffic

00:13:35.140 --> 00:13:37.860
but, because we're such a
large part of the internet,

00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:40.550
to the internet as a whole.

00:13:40.550 --> 00:13:42.550
And, of course, because
this is the network that

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:45.550
drives and powers your
traffic to customers,

00:13:45.550 --> 00:13:47.940
it'll help Google
Cloud customers as well

00:13:47.940 --> 00:13:51.850
and overall deliver a
better internet for cloud.

00:13:51.850 --> 00:13:53.770
That's enough about
our underlying network.

00:13:53.770 --> 00:13:55.603
I'm going to hand over
to my colleague Dave.

00:14:02.654 --> 00:14:04.810
DAVE PHILLIPS: Well, thank
you very much, Donald.

00:14:04.810 --> 00:14:06.460
My name is Dave, Dave Phillips.

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:08.400
I'm a colleague of Donald's.

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:10.350
So here to talk to you
about the performance

00:14:10.350 --> 00:14:12.180
we have on the Google network.

00:14:12.180 --> 00:14:15.130
I'll do that by talking about
three layers of performance.

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:15.630
Right.

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:18.450
We'll talk about
the overall network

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:22.290
software and the algorithms that
we use, you use to talk to us

00:14:22.290 --> 00:14:25.200
and we used to talk
between our own services.

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:27.420
We'll talk about
the physical layer

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:31.655
and how that applies directly
to you and gives you benefit.

00:14:31.655 --> 00:14:34.320
And we'll talk about the
Andromeda cloud layer,

00:14:34.320 --> 00:14:38.340
so where all of your
cloud services live.

00:14:38.340 --> 00:14:40.440
And the main message I
want you to take away

00:14:40.440 --> 00:14:44.250
is that really at every layer
of the network, every level

00:14:44.250 --> 00:14:47.100
of performance, there's
engineering teams within Google

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:50.670
working to optimize, get
a little bit of advantage,

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:54.280
make things faster, make
things more resilient,

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:56.710
and big investment all
the way through it.

00:14:56.710 --> 00:15:00.240
So in the last three years,
we've spent $30 billion

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:01.470
on infrastructure.

00:15:01.470 --> 00:15:05.490
That's three zero and
billion with a B. Sundar

00:15:05.490 --> 00:15:08.010
announced this year we're
spending $13 billion,

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:10.920
so one three and again
billion with a B.

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:15.390
So let's start to look
at some of these layers

00:15:15.390 --> 00:15:17.970
and the innovation
we have about them.

00:15:17.970 --> 00:15:18.510
OK.

00:15:18.510 --> 00:15:22.110
So maybe starting
off, let's just

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:24.260
give you a snapshot of
the timeline of some

00:15:24.260 --> 00:15:26.590
of the innovations we've had.

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:28.770
And I guess some of the
things you'll maybe notice

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:30.690
is that some of the
things in the timeline

00:15:30.690 --> 00:15:33.960
show up a few years after we've
had them live for a while.

00:15:33.960 --> 00:15:37.740
So there's stuff that's on the
timeline that you can't see,

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:39.340
that you'll see a
few years from now.

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:41.053
Somebody will have
this presentation

00:15:41.053 --> 00:15:42.220
and there'll be stuff there.

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:43.900
But that's just how it goes.

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:46.830
But a lot of the stuff that's
public right now, well, Donald

00:15:46.830 --> 00:15:48.540
talked about SDN.

00:15:48.540 --> 00:15:51.540
That's a real core
of what we do.

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:55.840
SDN is the central software
controlled way of doing things.

00:15:55.840 --> 00:15:58.050
Think about your modern
way of navigating

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:01.140
what maps when it's got
real time traffic versus how

00:16:01.140 --> 00:16:03.210
you used to use a
satnav 15 years ago

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:04.230
and it was a fixed map.

00:16:04.230 --> 00:16:05.760
And if everybody went
the same direction,

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:06.718
it was actually slower.

00:16:06.718 --> 00:16:07.740
Right.

00:16:07.740 --> 00:16:11.250
So that's SDN with
central software control.

00:16:11.250 --> 00:16:14.070
Our first iteration
of this was B4.

00:16:14.070 --> 00:16:15.930
This is our massive
backbone between all

00:16:15.930 --> 00:16:18.580
of our data centers.

00:16:18.580 --> 00:16:22.920
We then introduced our Jupiter,
which is our massive switching

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:25.410
fabric that goes within
our data centers,

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:29.040
makes all these tens, hundreds
of thousands of machines

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:32.880
act together as one
great big system.

00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:36.960
Andromeda, the network software
defined virtualization layer

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:40.950
that you guys use for
building up your cloud,

00:16:40.950 --> 00:16:43.800
and Espresso, which Donald
mentioned the fort pillar which

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:46.230
is taking that software
defined capability

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:48.390
all the way out to the edge.

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:51.915
Other things here, Maglev,
our software defined--

00:16:51.915 --> 00:16:54.720
I'm sorry, our load balancer.

00:16:54.720 --> 00:16:56.700
That's been in
place at Google now

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:59.430
for maybe more than 10 years
taking in all of our front end

00:16:59.430 --> 00:17:00.360
traffic.

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:03.780
And why all of these base
building block infrastructures

00:17:03.780 --> 00:17:07.079
are very important is we solved
these problems like Google

00:17:07.079 --> 00:17:11.460
to scale to the level we
needed to to be able to deliver

00:17:11.460 --> 00:17:12.960
traffic for Google.

00:17:12.960 --> 00:17:15.630
But these are the
same building blocks

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:18.569
that are in use for
the cloud products.

00:17:18.569 --> 00:17:20.760
You get to use these
same building blocks.

00:17:24.920 --> 00:17:27.170
I guess I should hold this
instead of putting it down,

00:17:27.170 --> 00:17:28.620
right?

00:17:28.620 --> 00:17:29.160
OK.

00:17:29.160 --> 00:17:32.860
So we said we'd talk
about these three layers.

00:17:32.860 --> 00:17:36.190
Let's talk about performance
at the network software layer.

00:17:36.190 --> 00:17:41.280
So as Donald mentioned, we want
to make the internet better

00:17:41.280 --> 00:17:45.300
as a whole, so not just
ourselves internally

00:17:45.300 --> 00:17:48.330
but overall, because we
make it better for everybody

00:17:48.330 --> 00:17:50.340
and everybody adopts
these standards,

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:52.180
everything gets faster.

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:53.970
So let me tell you
about two technologies

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:59.970
here I'll talk about BBR
and I'll talk about QUIC.

00:17:59.970 --> 00:18:02.470
So let's talk about BBR first.

00:18:02.470 --> 00:18:02.970
OK.

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:06.270
So this is an algorithm within--

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:10.550
a manner of implementing
congestion control within TCP.

00:18:10.550 --> 00:18:15.900
So TCP for the longest
time was loss-based.

00:18:15.900 --> 00:18:18.570
From the late 1980s, it's
pretty much been loss-based.

00:18:18.570 --> 00:18:21.190
Now that's worked
for a long time.

00:18:21.190 --> 00:18:24.720
But when you talk about modern
networks, which typically have

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:27.587
high capacity links but
relatively small buffers,

00:18:27.587 --> 00:18:29.670
actually this is not such
a great way of doing it.

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:31.770
When TCP algorithms
detect loss, they

00:18:31.770 --> 00:18:34.020
can back off quite sharply.

00:18:34.020 --> 00:18:35.460
And actually,
counterintuitively,

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:37.380
on very high capacity
networks, sometimes you

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:39.923
don't get such great
performance on that.

00:18:39.923 --> 00:18:41.840
So we need to rethink
that from the ground up.

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:44.640
BBR is a way of doing that.

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:51.510
BBR stands for bottleneck
bandwidth round trip

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:52.463
propagation time.

00:18:52.463 --> 00:18:53.880
It's a fundamentally
different way

00:18:53.880 --> 00:18:55.830
of looking at
congestion control.

00:18:55.830 --> 00:19:00.150
And what it does is detects
actual congestion and models

00:19:00.150 --> 00:19:02.880
throughput end to end
to be able to optimize

00:19:02.880 --> 00:19:04.910
rather than being loss-based.

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:08.180
It gives us a very, very
significant advantage.

00:19:08.180 --> 00:19:10.380
There's a nice
little graphic here.

00:19:10.380 --> 00:19:12.840
Because it's able to detect
the actual congestion

00:19:12.840 --> 00:19:15.690
rather than reacting very
aggressively to loss,

00:19:15.690 --> 00:19:17.750
you get much greater throughput.

00:19:17.750 --> 00:19:20.110
When we enabled this
on YouTube, overall

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:23.430
globally we got 4%
greater transmit

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:26.847
rate, 14% in some countries.

00:19:26.847 --> 00:19:29.430
These are actually big numbers,
because that was really highly

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:31.100
optimized to begin with.

00:19:31.100 --> 00:19:31.630
OK.

00:19:31.630 --> 00:19:34.105
Now very easy for
you to use that.

00:19:34.105 --> 00:19:35.730
As soon as you talk
from your front end

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:37.380
to your back ends
in Google Cloud,

00:19:37.380 --> 00:19:39.090
you're already using this.

00:19:39.090 --> 00:19:41.850
As soon as you go to anything
which terminates on the Google

00:19:41.850 --> 00:19:45.010
side, your sessions from then
on are automatically using BBR.

00:19:45.010 --> 00:19:45.510
Right.

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:48.720
So you get that.

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:53.010
So when you're doing
big transactions

00:19:53.010 --> 00:19:55.590
rather back to Google Cloud
Storage or anything like that,

00:19:55.590 --> 00:19:56.620
you're using this BBR.

00:19:56.620 --> 00:19:57.870
You're taking advantage of it.

00:19:57.870 --> 00:20:00.640
You're getting that throughput.

00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:01.590
Let's change tracks.

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:03.310
Let's talk about
another innovation.

00:20:03.310 --> 00:20:05.460
Let's talk about QUIC.

00:20:05.460 --> 00:20:14.160
QUIC is quick UDP
internet connections.

00:20:14.160 --> 00:20:18.570
And what this is doing is a
way of optimizing your setup so

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:23.740
where you would have used
HTTPS and TLS over TCP.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:24.790
OK.

00:20:24.790 --> 00:20:26.480
Now when you do
that traditionally,

00:20:26.480 --> 00:20:31.770
with using HTTPS using
TCP and TLS over TCP--

00:20:31.770 --> 00:20:33.810
sorry, over TCP--
you're typically

00:20:33.810 --> 00:20:35.640
taking about three
round trips to get

00:20:35.640 --> 00:20:39.470
that initial setup, three way
handshakes for that to happen.

00:20:39.470 --> 00:20:43.440
With QUIC, if you've already
talked with the server,

00:20:43.440 --> 00:20:46.060
you can begin transmitting
traffic immediately.

00:20:46.060 --> 00:20:46.560
Right.

00:20:46.560 --> 00:20:49.690
So very quick, especially
for that first page load.

00:20:49.690 --> 00:20:52.640
When we enable that,
we're seeing about 8%

00:20:52.640 --> 00:20:54.780
faster page load.

00:20:54.780 --> 00:20:56.700
Again, these were
highly optimized things

00:20:56.700 --> 00:20:59.610
to begin with so that's
actually a big number.

00:20:59.610 --> 00:21:02.280
How you enable
that yourselves is

00:21:02.280 --> 00:21:06.390
on your load balancer inside
the system you just enable QUIC.

00:21:06.390 --> 00:21:08.790
Very easy to do.

00:21:08.790 --> 00:21:10.160
QUIC is also very, very good.

00:21:10.160 --> 00:21:14.620
It was designed from the ground
up for multiplex streams.

00:21:14.620 --> 00:21:16.170
So traditionally
with TCP, if you

00:21:16.170 --> 00:21:18.630
have some multiplex streams,
you lose some traffic,

00:21:18.630 --> 00:21:20.340
all streams are held up.

00:21:20.340 --> 00:21:25.350
With QUIC, it's just the actual
stream that's lost the traffic.

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:26.630
The other streams are good.

00:21:26.630 --> 00:21:30.810
So also very good for
moving between wireless

00:21:30.810 --> 00:21:33.360
and mobile networks as
you may be doing here,

00:21:33.360 --> 00:21:37.110
because you have a connection
ID rather than a table.

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:40.770
So again, these are ways
we're optimizing things

00:21:40.770 --> 00:21:43.650
at the fundamental
software algorithm layer

00:21:43.650 --> 00:21:44.785
to make things faster.

00:21:48.780 --> 00:21:50.645
Let's talk about
that second thing

00:21:50.645 --> 00:21:52.770
I mentioned, which is let's
talk about improvements

00:21:52.770 --> 00:21:56.040
at the physical layer and
how they manifest to you.

00:21:56.040 --> 00:21:59.850
Well, so this graphic here
you may have seen some press

00:21:59.850 --> 00:22:01.410
about last week.

00:22:01.410 --> 00:22:06.720
This is our Dunant transatlantic
cable that we just announced.

00:22:06.720 --> 00:22:07.690
Donald mentioned this.

00:22:07.690 --> 00:22:10.110
So this is our
second private cable.

00:22:10.110 --> 00:22:14.070
We actually have 13
sub-sea cable investments.

00:22:14.070 --> 00:22:15.570
So what that means
for you, firstly,

00:22:15.570 --> 00:22:18.510
is that we we're going to have
that deterministic capacity

00:22:18.510 --> 00:22:21.720
available for you when you need
it with a lot of innovation

00:22:21.720 --> 00:22:23.580
at this layer.

00:22:23.580 --> 00:22:28.146
So this cable here is going
from Virginia to France.

00:22:28.146 --> 00:22:30.060
And normally when
you're in a data center

00:22:30.060 --> 00:22:33.780
and you see like a fiber
tray, or a duct, or a trench,

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:37.050
you see hundreds of cables
or thousands of fibers.

00:22:37.050 --> 00:22:37.990
Right.

00:22:37.990 --> 00:22:39.790
Subsea, it's very,
very different.

00:22:39.790 --> 00:22:42.750
The amount of power you need
to regenerate those signals

00:22:42.750 --> 00:22:44.880
means in a typical
subsea cable you

00:22:44.880 --> 00:22:49.710
have at most maybe six or
eight fiber pairs within that.

00:22:49.710 --> 00:22:52.320
This cable here, Dunant
cable, this uses something

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:54.110
called space division
multiplexing.

00:22:54.110 --> 00:22:56.870
And it's the first cable
undersea to do that.

00:22:56.870 --> 00:22:59.310
It means we can reuse
some of the same repeaters

00:22:59.310 --> 00:23:02.520
from multiple fiber pairs
instead of just one.

00:23:02.520 --> 00:23:05.310
So this system
here, this actually

00:23:05.310 --> 00:23:08.160
has 12 cables, world first.

00:23:08.160 --> 00:23:08.660
Right.

00:23:08.660 --> 00:23:10.950
So a lot of innovation
at that level right

00:23:10.950 --> 00:23:14.160
at the physical layer.

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:17.490
We name all of our cables
after famous scientists.

00:23:17.490 --> 00:23:19.170
So Curie.

00:23:19.170 --> 00:23:21.210
Dunant was the first
Nobel Prize winner.

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:23.970
The next one will be E and
so think of a scientist

00:23:23.970 --> 00:23:26.670
beginning with E. You can
guess what that will be.

00:23:26.670 --> 00:23:28.157
Maybe we can have a competition.

00:23:28.157 --> 00:23:30.740
I don't actually genuinely know
what the cable will be called.

00:23:30.740 --> 00:23:33.820
A and B where alphas and betas.

00:23:33.820 --> 00:23:35.100
OK.

00:23:35.100 --> 00:23:38.950
So linking back to that, so
why does all this matter?

00:23:38.950 --> 00:23:41.880
Why does having all this
physical infrastructure matter

00:23:41.880 --> 00:23:46.080
and give you tangible benefit?

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:52.590
Well, a good way of showing
this is our premium tier.

00:23:52.590 --> 00:23:55.390
So Donald had a really,
really good statistic,

00:23:55.390 --> 00:24:00.010
which is that 99% of our traffic
is being handed off directly,

00:24:00.010 --> 00:24:02.250
so really meaning
that it's going really

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:06.060
all the way across the
internet or across the span

00:24:06.060 --> 00:24:09.150
between source and destination
on the Google network,

00:24:09.150 --> 00:24:12.000
on this very, very fast
very high performance Google

00:24:12.000 --> 00:24:13.510
network.

00:24:13.510 --> 00:24:21.570
Now, our standard tier, this is
the way most cloud stuff works,

00:24:21.570 --> 00:24:23.430
most cloud provider works.

00:24:23.430 --> 00:24:25.838
If you're a routing person--

00:24:25.838 --> 00:24:27.630
I'm from Ireland so I
say a rooting person,

00:24:27.630 --> 00:24:29.640
but I've had to learn
to say routing person.

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:32.730
So if you're a
routing person, this

00:24:32.730 --> 00:24:36.180
is the difference between cold
potato and hot potato routing.

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:39.450
Cold potato routing is you
take the traffic all the way

00:24:39.450 --> 00:24:40.350
on your own network.

00:24:40.350 --> 00:24:42.480
That's a premium tier
hot potato is you hand it

00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:44.300
off as soon as possible.

00:24:44.300 --> 00:24:45.960
OK.

00:24:45.960 --> 00:24:47.580
This makes a really
big difference.

00:24:47.580 --> 00:24:49.470
If you have premium
tier enabled,

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:52.800
you're getting a ride
on the earliest ingress

00:24:52.800 --> 00:24:54.360
point on the Google network.

00:24:54.360 --> 00:24:56.795
And Donald showed you the scale
on all of those locations.

00:24:56.795 --> 00:24:58.920
You're coming all the way
across the Google network

00:24:58.920 --> 00:25:02.340
to your compute, then all the
way back across the Google

00:25:02.340 --> 00:25:04.310
network and then off.

00:25:04.310 --> 00:25:06.400
If you're doing
the standard tier,

00:25:06.400 --> 00:25:09.480
you're pretty much coming
in closest to your compute

00:25:09.480 --> 00:25:12.120
and then you're being handed
off back to the internet.

00:25:12.120 --> 00:25:14.370
And you're taking your chances
with the wider internet

00:25:14.370 --> 00:25:15.910
after that.

00:25:15.910 --> 00:25:18.240
Now what does this mean
in terms of performance?

00:25:18.240 --> 00:25:19.990
Well, it's a big deal.

00:25:19.990 --> 00:25:21.100
Don't take my word for it.

00:25:21.100 --> 00:25:22.050
I actually work for Google.

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:23.425
I'm going to tell
you it's great.

00:25:23.425 --> 00:25:28.050
But look at the actual external
folks that test this stuff.

00:25:28.050 --> 00:25:28.980
This is Cedexis.

00:25:28.980 --> 00:25:31.860
This is actually I pulled this
data just from last month.

00:25:31.860 --> 00:25:33.670
The graph doesn't
actually start at zero.

00:25:33.670 --> 00:25:34.670
It starts at three.

00:25:34.670 --> 00:25:39.720
So you're talking about 1.9x
greater throughput going

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:43.350
directly across that network
as opposed to going across

00:25:43.350 --> 00:25:45.630
and taking your chances
with the wider internet.

00:25:45.630 --> 00:25:47.040
And that's what
you would expect.

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:49.110
If we build is really,
really high performance

00:25:49.110 --> 00:25:51.600
network with all these fibers,
and all this cable, and all

00:25:51.600 --> 00:25:54.520
this reach, you're going to
get much better performance.

00:25:54.520 --> 00:25:55.830
So that's the premium tier.

00:25:55.830 --> 00:26:00.180
Now very easy for
you to enable that.

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:02.990
Simply inside your
console on your VPC

00:26:02.990 --> 00:26:05.190
is you're just going
to enable premium tier.

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:07.830
You don't need to go messing
with all this fancy routing.

00:26:07.830 --> 00:26:10.230
And that's a big
message here that we're

00:26:10.230 --> 00:26:13.410
able to make all of this
technology very, very

00:26:13.410 --> 00:26:15.660
easy for you to access.

00:26:15.660 --> 00:26:18.042
It's very complex at our
end to make it all work.

00:26:18.042 --> 00:26:20.250
I actually worked in networking
for the last 10 years

00:26:20.250 --> 00:26:21.580
before I came over to product.

00:26:21.580 --> 00:26:22.450
Right.

00:26:22.450 --> 00:26:25.545
But in terms of how it works
for you guys as customers,

00:26:25.545 --> 00:26:29.180
it's very, very easy for you
to access this and turn it on.

00:26:29.180 --> 00:26:30.930
So let me just put
three of those products

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:32.650
together as an example.

00:26:32.650 --> 00:26:35.010
Let's talk about that
premium networking.

00:26:35.010 --> 00:26:36.880
Let's talk about VPC.

00:26:36.880 --> 00:26:39.040
And let's talk about
global load balancing.

00:26:39.040 --> 00:26:39.540
OK.

00:26:39.540 --> 00:26:41.783
VPC is your virtual
private cloud.

00:26:41.783 --> 00:26:43.450
I'll talk about that
a little bit later,

00:26:43.450 --> 00:26:45.480
but essentially this
is your main network

00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:48.330
construct that you build, one
of the first things that you

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:49.110
build.

00:26:49.110 --> 00:26:52.650
Here's something to know
is that that is inherently

00:26:52.650 --> 00:26:55.130
a global construct.

00:26:55.130 --> 00:26:55.980
Right.

00:26:55.980 --> 00:26:58.230
You might use it on a
regional basis initially

00:26:58.230 --> 00:27:01.660
but that's inherently
a global construct.

00:27:01.660 --> 00:27:04.890
Now let's talk about
global load balancing.

00:27:04.890 --> 00:27:09.630
When you enable this global load
balancing at Google, what we do

00:27:09.630 --> 00:27:12.840
is we give your service
a virtual IP address.

00:27:12.840 --> 00:27:15.950
But then instead of having
to play around with DNS

00:27:15.950 --> 00:27:18.790
or doing anything like that,
we do this a much better way,

00:27:18.790 --> 00:27:19.990
which is we do anycast.

00:27:19.990 --> 00:27:23.070
So from all of our edge
bots on our extensive edge

00:27:23.070 --> 00:27:26.562
infrastructure, we will
anycast out that address,

00:27:26.562 --> 00:27:28.395
meaning that that same
address is advertised

00:27:28.395 --> 00:27:30.750
in all these different
regions, meaning that you will

00:27:30.750 --> 00:27:33.720
be pulled in at the
most proximate location

00:27:33.720 --> 00:27:35.470
on our global network.

00:27:35.470 --> 00:27:36.360
Right.

00:27:36.360 --> 00:27:38.070
So here's an example.

00:27:38.070 --> 00:27:41.460
We have Maya in California.

00:27:41.460 --> 00:27:44.130
She has pulled in
directly to the US West,

00:27:44.130 --> 00:27:46.740
which is on the west coast.

00:27:46.740 --> 00:27:51.060
We have Bob in London going
to the same address which

00:27:51.060 --> 00:27:54.060
you have your map to your
primary DNS A record.

00:27:54.060 --> 00:27:56.875
And he's been sucked
into the London site,

00:27:56.875 --> 00:27:59.820
so for very fast
local performance.

00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:02.820
And then you have Shen
in Singapore again

00:28:02.820 --> 00:28:08.200
using the same IP address DNS
going into the Singapore pop

00:28:08.200 --> 00:28:08.700
there.

00:28:08.700 --> 00:28:11.110
That's what global
load balancing does.

00:28:11.110 --> 00:28:15.290
Now, because that
VPC is also global,

00:28:15.290 --> 00:28:17.070
it's able to do this
very clever stuff,

00:28:17.070 --> 00:28:20.910
like know if certain aspects
of your system are overloaded,

00:28:20.910 --> 00:28:23.890
it can automatically redirect
to others and overflow.

00:28:23.890 --> 00:28:24.780
Right.

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:27.480
So very, very clever and
very, very easy for you

00:28:27.480 --> 00:28:30.810
to use the power of this
enormous network turning it

00:28:30.810 --> 00:28:31.980
on like that.

00:28:31.980 --> 00:28:33.750
Let's take this diagram here.

00:28:33.750 --> 00:28:36.320
And let's imagine
we rewind it a bit.

00:28:36.320 --> 00:28:37.410
OK.

00:28:37.410 --> 00:28:39.220
So I don't have an
animation or anything,

00:28:39.220 --> 00:28:42.090
but let's just pretend
we're not at a stage

00:28:42.090 --> 00:28:45.280
where we have US,
Europe, and Asia.

00:28:45.280 --> 00:28:47.580
Let's just say
we've just set up.

00:28:47.580 --> 00:28:49.200
Just to begin with,
we've just set up

00:28:49.200 --> 00:28:51.690
with a few VMs on
the west coast.

00:28:51.690 --> 00:28:52.770
OK.

00:28:52.770 --> 00:28:57.120
So what I can do, turn it on,
do this global load balancing,

00:28:57.120 --> 00:28:59.700
set up my VPC.

00:28:59.700 --> 00:29:02.520
And what's happening there is,
well, just all of the traffic

00:29:02.520 --> 00:29:04.460
has been sucked
into the west coast.

00:29:04.460 --> 00:29:05.550
All right.

00:29:05.550 --> 00:29:08.640
Then what I decide is
I'm really growing here.

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:10.680
I could really use a
presence in Europe,

00:29:10.680 --> 00:29:12.880
or the east coast, or wherever.

00:29:12.880 --> 00:29:14.910
So I set up in Europe.

00:29:14.910 --> 00:29:18.180
Now, because the VPC is global,
because global load balancing

00:29:18.180 --> 00:29:21.870
is global, just by setting
up in Europe automatically

00:29:21.870 --> 00:29:24.320
all that European traffic
or all the traffic proximate

00:29:24.320 --> 00:29:27.240
to that region is
being pulled in there.

00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:29.150
And then similarly with
that growth in Asia.

00:29:29.150 --> 00:29:34.020
So very easy to make use of this
without very advanced knowledge

00:29:34.020 --> 00:29:35.813
of the underlying
infrastructure.

00:29:39.420 --> 00:29:42.360
The other pillar I
wanted to talk about

00:29:42.360 --> 00:29:45.690
is performance at
the Andromeda layer.

00:29:45.690 --> 00:29:49.080
So this is the network
virtualization layer

00:29:49.080 --> 00:29:51.870
that all of this
stuff works within.

00:29:51.870 --> 00:29:53.190
OK.

00:29:53.190 --> 00:29:56.610
So this is this layer on
top of our infrastructure

00:29:56.610 --> 00:29:59.130
that's going to give you that
performance and functional

00:29:59.130 --> 00:30:04.300
isolation where your
infrastructure lives.

00:30:04.300 --> 00:30:04.800
OK.

00:30:07.410 --> 00:30:09.960
Let's first talk about
this idea of a VPC

00:30:09.960 --> 00:30:12.280
or a virtual private cloud.

00:30:12.280 --> 00:30:15.670
This is the basic building
block of your infrastructure.

00:30:15.670 --> 00:30:19.190
So this is what you define.

00:30:19.190 --> 00:30:20.880
And what you could
think about there's

00:30:20.880 --> 00:30:23.550
something similar to a network
you set up in your data center.

00:30:23.550 --> 00:30:27.750
This is your own little
private slice of the cloud.

00:30:27.750 --> 00:30:29.910
And a good thing about this
as, firstly, as I said,

00:30:29.910 --> 00:30:30.900
it's not region locked.

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:31.680
It's global.

00:30:31.680 --> 00:30:33.600
Now, if you have
a situation where

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:35.447
you're in financial
services or medical

00:30:35.447 --> 00:30:37.530
and you need your stuff
contained within a region,

00:30:37.530 --> 00:30:39.970
yes, absolutely we
can do that as well.

00:30:39.970 --> 00:30:41.670
But the concept
itself is inherently

00:30:41.670 --> 00:30:44.520
not something you need
to define in each region.

00:30:44.520 --> 00:30:48.750
Replicating that config,
you can define it once.

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:51.210
You also don't need a
VPC for every project.

00:30:51.210 --> 00:30:53.250
You can do this
idea of a shared VPC

00:30:53.250 --> 00:30:56.450
where you've got a
services VPC and then

00:30:56.450 --> 00:31:00.030
you got host
projects off of that.

00:31:00.030 --> 00:31:02.880
So very easy way to maintain
one common security model,

00:31:02.880 --> 00:31:06.300
one common administrative model.

00:31:06.300 --> 00:31:09.630
All of your traffic within
that VPC is very, very secure.

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:13.160
It's not touching the public
network, the public internet.

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:17.160
And, as Donald said, you have
all those 134 plus points

00:31:17.160 --> 00:31:18.750
which you can interconnect.

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:19.890
OK.

00:31:19.890 --> 00:31:22.950
So common overall
model using this

00:31:22.950 --> 00:31:27.290
VPC as the base
networking block.

00:31:27.290 --> 00:31:28.540
Oh, wrong button.

00:31:31.110 --> 00:31:32.410
So this is what it does.

00:31:32.410 --> 00:31:34.590
This is what the Andromeda does.

00:31:34.590 --> 00:31:37.530
It essentially takes all of
our huge infrastructure, all

00:31:37.530 --> 00:31:41.550
those data centers, all
those fibers, all those pops,

00:31:41.550 --> 00:31:44.520
and chops it up so that you
have your own slice of it.

00:31:44.520 --> 00:31:49.570
It provides that security and
that functional and performance

00:31:49.570 --> 00:31:50.360
isolation.

00:31:50.360 --> 00:31:53.500
So here we can see we've
got the green customer,

00:31:53.500 --> 00:31:55.620
yellow customer, and
the red customer.

00:31:55.620 --> 00:31:57.520
They're all using
10 space addresses

00:31:57.520 --> 00:32:00.540
but that doesn't matter because
they're all completely isolated

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:03.820
and all working independently
within this Andromeda layer

00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:05.585
using our infrastructure.

00:32:10.810 --> 00:32:11.540
OK.

00:32:11.540 --> 00:32:12.870
So how does it work?

00:32:12.870 --> 00:32:16.730
Well, in terms of
what we do is we

00:32:16.730 --> 00:32:20.310
push a lot of the networking
all the way to the host.

00:32:20.310 --> 00:32:23.218
In fact, we do
that in user space.

00:32:23.218 --> 00:32:25.010
We push all the way
down to the user space.

00:32:25.010 --> 00:32:26.700
Why do we do that?

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:29.870
Well, firstly, it's very secure.

00:32:29.870 --> 00:32:32.810
The data plane is
not running in route.

00:32:32.810 --> 00:32:35.600
Secondly, it's a
very low footprint

00:32:35.600 --> 00:32:38.676
so we can really
test it, fuzz it.

00:32:38.676 --> 00:32:41.570
A very small surface
for any kind of security

00:32:41.570 --> 00:32:42.470
vulnerabilities.

00:32:42.470 --> 00:32:44.630
Very high performance
in a single processor.

00:32:44.630 --> 00:32:47.150
We're getting three
billion packets per second.

00:32:47.150 --> 00:32:51.530
For core processors then
we'll do more advanced packet

00:32:51.530 --> 00:32:53.140
rerouting and things like that.

00:32:53.140 --> 00:32:55.010
But a fast pat through
a single processor

00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:57.440
gives us this very,
very high performance

00:32:57.440 --> 00:33:00.980
without needing to rely on any
ASICs or any specific hardware.

00:33:00.980 --> 00:33:04.100
It allows us to
iterate very quickly.

00:33:04.100 --> 00:33:06.260
Allows us to do
hit list upgrades.

00:33:06.260 --> 00:33:09.170
Allows us to do live migrations.

00:33:09.170 --> 00:33:12.580
Allows us to continually
innovate and push features.

00:33:16.530 --> 00:33:19.560
This is the overall
architecture itself.

00:33:19.560 --> 00:33:23.040
The control plane itself
was built as a hierarchy.

00:33:23.040 --> 00:33:26.220
You have a cloud
cluster manager.

00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:28.440
You then have a fabric
management layer.

00:33:28.440 --> 00:33:30.720
You then have the
open flow layer,

00:33:30.720 --> 00:33:34.740
which we push the configuration
down to at an SDN level.

00:33:37.440 --> 00:33:39.240
So fully replicated.

00:33:39.240 --> 00:33:43.100
Fully sharded across
zones, across regions.

00:33:43.100 --> 00:33:46.800
And by using that SDN and by
pushing the configuration down

00:33:46.800 --> 00:33:49.320
into the fabric, that
allows us to really do

00:33:49.320 --> 00:33:52.460
this horizontal scaling.

00:33:52.460 --> 00:33:53.580
And that's a very--

00:33:53.580 --> 00:33:55.080
let's think about it like this.

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:57.390
Think about our
internal load balancer.

00:33:57.390 --> 00:33:58.072
Right.

00:33:58.072 --> 00:34:00.030
Now we have all these
different load balancers.

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:01.860
We talked about the global
load balancer earlier.

00:34:01.860 --> 00:34:03.818
I'm now talking about an
internal load balancer

00:34:03.818 --> 00:34:06.837
within your slice
of the data center.

00:34:06.837 --> 00:34:08.670
Actually, how you
normally implement that is

00:34:08.670 --> 00:34:10.830
you have a dedicated box
or a dedicated machine

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:13.050
that does that or a
dedicated VM that does that.

00:34:13.050 --> 00:34:14.850
We don't.

00:34:14.850 --> 00:34:17.730
It's all existing within
the switch fabric.

00:34:17.730 --> 00:34:19.469
So it means there's
no middle box.

00:34:19.469 --> 00:34:20.610
There's no choke point.

00:34:20.610 --> 00:34:23.699
It's configuration pushed
out into the SDN layer which

00:34:23.699 --> 00:34:26.670
gives you that load balancing
capability so it gives you

00:34:26.670 --> 00:34:28.275
this near infinite scaling.

00:34:31.219 --> 00:34:33.870
This is an idea of some of
the level of performance

00:34:33.870 --> 00:34:37.350
we've gotten with
Andromeda over the years.

00:34:37.350 --> 00:34:39.350
So one of the main things
we've tried to look at

00:34:39.350 --> 00:34:42.679
is the cycles per byte in
terms of transmitting traffic.

00:34:42.679 --> 00:34:46.159
As we've gone through the
various iterations there,

00:34:46.159 --> 00:34:49.350
we've had very, very significant
levels of improvement up to 16x

00:34:49.350 --> 00:34:53.300
and up to 50 byte per
cycle improvement.

00:34:53.300 --> 00:34:55.790
The biggest jump was
to Andromeda 2.0.

00:34:55.790 --> 00:34:58.940
And we put most everything
into the guest layer,

00:34:58.940 --> 00:35:00.890
bypassing the host.

00:35:00.890 --> 00:35:03.110
Andromeda 2.1 we got
rid of the last tread.

00:35:03.110 --> 00:35:07.840
And Andromeda 2.2 we
added memory copying.

00:35:07.840 --> 00:35:10.910
And we continue to
innovate very aggressively.

00:35:10.910 --> 00:35:13.910
Just since we talked to you
last at the last Cloud Next,

00:35:13.910 --> 00:35:19.640
let's see we've introduced NPLM,
longest prefix matching caches

00:35:19.640 --> 00:35:20.400
per machine.

00:35:20.400 --> 00:35:23.600
So for internet heavy workloads,
that makes a big difference.

00:35:23.600 --> 00:35:26.270
Our Hermes system for
network configuration

00:35:26.270 --> 00:35:29.150
pushes is now twice
as fast, about 15

00:35:29.150 --> 00:35:32.360
seconds to push the network
config versus 30 seconds

00:35:32.360 --> 00:35:33.920
a year ago.

00:35:33.920 --> 00:35:37.430
We've dealt with 2018 was
there was a lot of very, very

00:35:37.430 --> 00:35:39.250
large CPU issues.

00:35:39.250 --> 00:35:42.200
Specter, mind meld, et cetera.

00:35:42.200 --> 00:35:44.280
We were able to deal
with those very,

00:35:44.280 --> 00:35:48.620
very easily in the
background from you.

00:35:48.620 --> 00:35:51.230
A lot of scaling in
our load balancers,

00:35:51.230 --> 00:35:53.808
particularly using something
called hoverboards.

00:35:53.808 --> 00:35:55.850
So we're able to scale
those load balancers quite

00:35:55.850 --> 00:35:57.472
a lot further than way were.

00:35:57.472 --> 00:35:59.930
And look, one thing that all
these improvements are leading

00:35:59.930 --> 00:36:03.770
to, higher performance
devices for you,

00:36:03.770 --> 00:36:05.780
higher performance services.

00:36:05.780 --> 00:36:08.390
So at Next here,
we'll be announcing

00:36:08.390 --> 00:36:11.390
the 32 gigabit per second VMs.

00:36:11.390 --> 00:36:15.260
Limit before this was
16 gig per second.

00:36:15.260 --> 00:36:18.440
Going all the way up to 32
now in your Skylake machine,

00:36:18.440 --> 00:36:20.390
that's in all regions.

00:36:20.390 --> 00:36:23.630
With 16 VCPUs, you get
all the way up to 32.

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:28.190
And that network is going to
give you that performance.

00:36:28.190 --> 00:36:34.950
Up to 100 gigs on our GPU level
machines too going into alpha.

00:36:34.950 --> 00:36:37.280
So if you have one of these
high end GPU machines,

00:36:37.280 --> 00:36:42.290
you want 100 gig for that,
talk to your account rep.

00:36:42.290 --> 00:36:44.930
Now let's look at how
we manage and monitor

00:36:44.930 --> 00:36:46.430
all of those solutions.

00:36:46.430 --> 00:36:48.410
I'll hand you over to
my colleague Manasa.

