MANASA CHALASANI:
My name is Manasa.

00:36:59.660 --> 00:37:00.350
OK.

00:37:00.350 --> 00:37:00.850
Here we go.

00:37:00.850 --> 00:37:02.080
My name is Manasa.

00:37:02.080 --> 00:37:05.050
And I'm going to
talk to you about how

00:37:05.050 --> 00:37:06.790
we monitor our network.

00:37:06.790 --> 00:37:10.570
You heard from
Donald earlier about

00:37:10.570 --> 00:37:13.210
how we build our global
network for resilience.

00:37:13.210 --> 00:37:17.080
And you heard from Dave about
our investments and innovations

00:37:17.080 --> 00:37:20.110
to be able to drive
for better performance.

00:37:20.110 --> 00:37:21.940
And I'll talk to you
about how we monitor

00:37:21.940 --> 00:37:24.460
our internal
infrastructure to begin

00:37:24.460 --> 00:37:27.700
with where Google has
a way of monitoring

00:37:27.700 --> 00:37:31.470
our network at various levels
in starting from intrazone,

00:37:31.470 --> 00:37:34.340
interzone, inter-VM,
to the edge, and so on.

00:37:34.340 --> 00:37:36.490
But we'll also
talk through how we

00:37:36.490 --> 00:37:40.600
think about monitoring how
our end users experience

00:37:40.600 --> 00:37:43.390
the services that get
deployed in Google Cloud.

00:37:43.390 --> 00:37:45.430
And then we'll
also go through how

00:37:45.430 --> 00:37:47.200
we use all these
metrics that get

00:37:47.200 --> 00:37:51.280
generated to be able to
optimize our service delivery

00:37:51.280 --> 00:37:57.590
parts to our end customers and
plan for the future as well.

00:37:57.590 --> 00:38:00.410
So, with that,
let's get started.

00:38:00.410 --> 00:38:03.550
Every machine on
the Google network

00:38:03.550 --> 00:38:05.680
is actually a part of an
overarching monitoring

00:38:05.680 --> 00:38:06.850
infrastructure.

00:38:06.850 --> 00:38:11.800
We collect near real time
logs on each of these cores

00:38:11.800 --> 00:38:12.580
that we have.

00:38:12.580 --> 00:38:16.690
So it includes metrics
like RTT and throughput.

00:38:16.690 --> 00:38:20.980
And this does not require
any additional software

00:38:20.980 --> 00:38:22.590
to get installed in
any of the hosts,

00:38:22.590 --> 00:38:26.200
so it comes at no additional
performance impact to you.

00:38:26.200 --> 00:38:31.720
What this enables us to do is
it enables us to monitor and get

00:38:31.720 --> 00:38:33.790
visibility into
our infrastructure

00:38:33.790 --> 00:38:35.360
at various levels.

00:38:35.360 --> 00:38:38.560
We can get metrics at the
VPC network as a whole,

00:38:38.560 --> 00:38:41.560
or for a specific subnet,
or drill down all the way

00:38:41.560 --> 00:38:46.140
to a specific VM instance
on a virtual interface.

00:38:46.140 --> 00:38:47.890
Apart from this
collection infrastructure,

00:38:47.890 --> 00:38:52.480
we've also deployed probes
extensively across our network.

00:38:52.480 --> 00:38:55.750
And this provides us with
additional visibility

00:38:55.750 --> 00:38:59.767
into things like
packet loss, latency,

00:38:59.767 --> 00:39:00.850
and various other metrics.

00:39:03.780 --> 00:39:06.070
So Google has overall
built a network

00:39:06.070 --> 00:39:09.460
where the network
and the host actually

00:39:09.460 --> 00:39:13.700
play well together to be able
to optimize for performance.

00:39:13.700 --> 00:39:18.070
We built this centralized
bandwidth allocation algorithm

00:39:18.070 --> 00:39:22.760
that's able to actually enforce
policy at the host level.

00:39:22.760 --> 00:39:25.983
And all of this works at
the scale of Google land.

00:39:25.983 --> 00:39:28.150
For those of you who are
interested in learning more

00:39:28.150 --> 00:39:30.440
about that, check
out the publication

00:39:30.440 --> 00:39:34.270
that we have called
bandwidth enforcer.

00:39:34.270 --> 00:39:37.890
And you should be able to
get to know more about that.

00:39:37.890 --> 00:39:38.390
OK.

00:39:38.390 --> 00:39:43.000
Moving forward, like I mentioned
let's talk a little bit more

00:39:43.000 --> 00:39:47.560
about how we think about
understanding how our end

00:39:47.560 --> 00:39:51.190
users experience services that
get deployed on Google Cloud,

00:39:51.190 --> 00:39:53.073
because from an end
user point of view,

00:39:53.073 --> 00:39:55.240
they don't really care where
the network issues are.

00:39:55.240 --> 00:39:55.980
Right.

00:39:55.980 --> 00:39:59.740
It might be at the Google end
or external to our network,

00:39:59.740 --> 00:40:01.510
but they just want
things to work.

00:40:01.510 --> 00:40:03.550
So we at Google need
to think about it more

00:40:03.550 --> 00:40:08.320
proactively to understand
where the issue lies

00:40:08.320 --> 00:40:10.300
and work with our
partners proactively

00:40:10.300 --> 00:40:12.470
to be able to
manage those issues.

00:40:12.470 --> 00:40:14.500
So we've built an
infrastructure in technology

00:40:14.500 --> 00:40:19.450
where we're able to understand
connectivity issues from end

00:40:19.450 --> 00:40:23.050
users coming into
Google's network itself

00:40:23.050 --> 00:40:25.360
and be able to attribute
that specifically

00:40:25.360 --> 00:40:28.660
to where that issue lies,
be it at Google's end

00:40:28.660 --> 00:40:31.450
or outside of Google's network.

00:40:31.450 --> 00:40:35.770
And that enables us to
work with our partners

00:40:35.770 --> 00:40:40.690
and minimize the impact to
end users, be it our end users

00:40:40.690 --> 00:40:43.690
or our cloud
customers' end users.

00:40:43.690 --> 00:40:48.300
And as you can
imagine, a lot of--

00:40:48.300 --> 00:40:51.760
we serve about a billion
plus users with this network.

00:40:51.760 --> 00:40:54.360
And they are dispersed
geographically.

00:40:54.360 --> 00:40:56.260
So we're collecting
all of these metrics

00:40:56.260 --> 00:40:59.300
from all corners
of the internet.

00:40:59.300 --> 00:41:02.080
So what do we use
these metrics for?

00:41:02.080 --> 00:41:03.960
First and foremost,
we've built models

00:41:03.960 --> 00:41:06.820
goes to be able to
detect and alert

00:41:06.820 --> 00:41:09.880
about fault conditions
or near fault conditions.

00:41:09.880 --> 00:41:12.370
And then we take
all this data to be

00:41:12.370 --> 00:41:16.270
able to provide a feedback
loop and optimize our service

00:41:16.270 --> 00:41:19.520
delivery paths to our customers.

00:41:19.520 --> 00:41:21.640
And we also then
take this data to be

00:41:21.640 --> 00:41:26.110
able to minimize the time
we spend on troubleshooting

00:41:26.110 --> 00:41:28.900
and mitigating issues.

00:41:28.900 --> 00:41:32.230
And over a longer time,
we collect these patterns

00:41:32.230 --> 00:41:35.530
and understand these patterns to
be able to plan for the future

00:41:35.530 --> 00:41:39.850
and understand to be able
to plan our traffic growth

00:41:39.850 --> 00:41:43.910
and capacity as we go forward.

00:41:43.910 --> 00:41:47.150
So we talked about the
metrics that we collect.

00:41:47.150 --> 00:41:49.588
We talked about how we use them.

00:41:49.588 --> 00:41:51.130
But let's talk a
little bit about how

00:41:51.130 --> 00:41:54.910
we measure these metrics.

00:41:54.910 --> 00:41:57.430
Over the past few years,
as platform density

00:41:57.430 --> 00:42:01.150
has increased to go
to include thousands

00:42:01.150 --> 00:42:05.410
of physical interfaces, and
as the deployments themselves

00:42:05.410 --> 00:42:08.230
have changed, we have
started to collect metrics

00:42:08.230 --> 00:42:11.560
that the number of metrics have
actually grown exponentially.

00:42:11.560 --> 00:42:12.280
Right.

00:42:12.280 --> 00:42:15.560
So traditional protocol
based methods like SNMP

00:42:15.560 --> 00:42:20.680
and syslog don't really
work much anymore.

00:42:20.680 --> 00:42:24.340
For example, a typical
dynamically provisioned app

00:42:24.340 --> 00:42:27.280
could have been spun
up, served its purpose,

00:42:27.280 --> 00:42:32.140
and been shut down well
within a five minute time

00:42:32.140 --> 00:42:35.830
span that is included in
the SNMP polling cycle.

00:42:35.830 --> 00:42:39.280
So that makes our network
operators run blind.

00:42:39.280 --> 00:42:41.540
So we needed a better solution.

00:42:41.540 --> 00:42:43.630
So what we did was we
worked with industry leaders

00:42:43.630 --> 00:42:45.670
like Arista, Cisco,
Juniper to be

00:42:45.670 --> 00:42:48.820
able to advance the state of
the art in device monitoring.

00:42:48.820 --> 00:42:52.420
We have been working to
build and standardize

00:42:52.420 --> 00:42:57.070
streaming telemetry as
a replacement for SNMP

00:42:57.070 --> 00:42:58.990
and syslog like architectures.

00:42:58.990 --> 00:43:02.480
And streaming
telemetry, what it does

00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:07.570
is it provides a high fidelity
data in near real time

00:43:07.570 --> 00:43:12.370
performance to be able to help
us optimize for our traffic

00:43:12.370 --> 00:43:15.100
bots and provide more real
time performance metrics.

00:43:18.120 --> 00:43:22.820
We also work with
ISPs is worldwide

00:43:22.820 --> 00:43:26.450
to be able to provide them
with near real time performance

00:43:26.450 --> 00:43:27.830
measurements.

00:43:27.830 --> 00:43:31.100
Google maintains a global
footprint with a generally open

00:43:31.100 --> 00:43:32.600
peering policy.

00:43:32.600 --> 00:43:36.230
And we provide access
to the Google ISP portal

00:43:36.230 --> 00:43:41.420
to operators who share
the direct network edge

00:43:41.420 --> 00:43:43.940
relationship with us.

00:43:43.940 --> 00:43:47.390
With this ISP portal, what
our operatives are able to do

00:43:47.390 --> 00:43:51.890
is they are able to get
insights about how exactly

00:43:51.890 --> 00:43:54.290
Google's sending
traffic and why that's

00:43:54.290 --> 00:43:57.590
the case to those specific
partners, how we together

00:43:57.590 --> 00:44:02.570
can grow, and specific insights
into actionable metrics

00:44:02.570 --> 00:44:06.200
actually into how
to improve delivery

00:44:06.200 --> 00:44:09.630
quality, and performance,
and things like that.

00:44:09.630 --> 00:44:12.260
So what you see here
is actually a part

00:44:12.260 --> 00:44:15.350
of a snapshot of our
Google ISP portal.

00:44:15.350 --> 00:44:21.320
And you can see that what
we provide is basically

00:44:21.320 --> 00:44:24.470
ingress and egress
traffic breakdowns,

00:44:24.470 --> 00:44:31.790
performance metrics like RTT,
throughput, even video quality,

00:44:31.790 --> 00:44:35.840
and all the way go down
to providing our partners

00:44:35.840 --> 00:44:38.910
with capabilities to be able
to actually predict what

00:44:38.910 --> 00:44:40.160
the traffic growth looks like.

00:44:43.120 --> 00:44:46.500
So we've also been--

00:44:46.500 --> 00:44:51.000
we also have ongoing
investments on the product end

00:44:51.000 --> 00:44:54.060
where we want to share more
and more of these metrics

00:44:54.060 --> 00:44:56.670
with our cloud customers.

00:44:56.670 --> 00:45:01.420
I'll talk through a few
of these products today.

00:45:01.420 --> 00:45:04.170
The first one of them is
load balancing telemetry.

00:45:04.170 --> 00:45:08.910
So load balance and
monitoring helps

00:45:08.910 --> 00:45:12.840
you get visibility into your
internet facing applications

00:45:12.840 --> 00:45:15.570
and perform diagnostics
around that.

00:45:15.570 --> 00:45:18.690
It includes measurements
like number of connections,

00:45:18.690 --> 00:45:21.630
latency for both the front
end and the back end,

00:45:21.630 --> 00:45:24.420
and throughput.

00:45:24.420 --> 00:45:26.970
Let's talk about VPC flow logs.

00:45:26.970 --> 00:45:30.390
Think of VPC flow logs
as equivalent to NetFlow

00:45:30.390 --> 00:45:32.460
for GPC environments.

00:45:32.460 --> 00:45:34.650
We are able to monitor
everything that comes

00:45:34.650 --> 00:45:36.950
in and out of the compute.

00:45:36.950 --> 00:45:39.890
It's near real time without
any performance impact to you

00:45:39.890 --> 00:45:43.380
when you turn these on
in your GCP environments.

00:45:43.380 --> 00:45:47.940
And you can again measure
things at the VPC network level

00:45:47.940 --> 00:45:51.690
or drill down all the way
to the VM instance level.

00:45:51.690 --> 00:45:53.460
Firewall logs.

00:45:53.460 --> 00:45:56.760
So, basically, firewall logs
help you to get visibility

00:45:56.760 --> 00:46:01.515
into every connection attempt
against a firewall rule that

00:46:01.515 --> 00:46:02.730
had logging enabled.

00:46:02.730 --> 00:46:06.090
So you will be able to
audit or perform forensics

00:46:06.090 --> 00:46:11.760
on all the connections
that got denied or allowed

00:46:11.760 --> 00:46:15.070
over a longer period of
time to figure out patterns.

00:46:15.070 --> 00:46:18.660
And you can consume all of
this information in Stackdriver

00:46:18.660 --> 00:46:22.650
or export it to some of
the third party tools

00:46:22.650 --> 00:46:25.360
that we have deployed
today in the environments.

00:46:25.360 --> 00:46:28.630
We have integrations with
some of the major partners,

00:46:28.630 --> 00:46:31.720
including Splunk, Sumo
Logic, and many more.

00:46:31.720 --> 00:46:34.920
You can go check those out.

00:46:34.920 --> 00:46:39.263
So in summary, you heard
about our resilient network.

00:46:39.263 --> 00:46:41.430
You heard about the innovations
and investments here

00:46:41.430 --> 00:46:43.620
and how we monitor it.

00:46:43.620 --> 00:46:47.010
Here are a few of
the other networking

00:46:47.010 --> 00:46:49.050
sessions that you might
be interested in that

00:46:49.050 --> 00:46:51.750
are relevant that are
going to take place

00:46:51.750 --> 00:46:53.670
over the next few
days and a link

00:46:53.670 --> 00:46:56.250
to our networking
blog in case you're

00:46:56.250 --> 00:46:59.100
interested in following us for
any of the future announcements

00:46:59.100 --> 00:46:59.860
in the space.

00:46:59.860 --> 00:47:03.210
[MUSIC PLAYING]

